{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310733,"nonce":"SBq0qGdK+768ZanFKrCOWwVZnAo=","title":"test_exception was NOT flaky — it was THREE deterministic bugs, all FIXED. (1) 78f840ee: static-abstract-method inliner had no recursion ...","message":"User flagged (correctly) that test_exception \"used to be a flake but now happens every time\" and \"we should no longer overlook\" it. Measured: test_exception 0/20 PASS (deterministic), test_copy 12/8 of 20 (STILL genuinely flaky — that one IS the W0020 trap-stub class). So test_exception ≠ flaky; it's a deterministic stack overflow. Diagnosed via lldb (`bt`) + a thread-local depth guard in lower_expression + a func_id counter in rayzor_push_call_frame + RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR.\n\n`try_inline_static_abstract_method` (tast_to_hir.rs) had NO recursion guard. haxe.CallStack's `equalItems`/`itemToString` are STATIC abstract methods that recurse on the nested `StackItem.FilePos(s:Null\u003cStackItem\u003e)` argument. Inlining them expanded forever → overflow during TAST→HIR lowering. The whole abstract is lowered on IMPORT, so `import haxe.CallStack.StackItem; StackItem.CFunction` overflowed even though those methods were unused → ANY program touching haxe.CallStack/NativeStackTrace failed to compile. lldb cycle: lower_expression → inline_expression_deep → inline_expression_deep → try_inline_static_abstract_method → (repeat). Fix = thread-local HashSet\u003cSymbolId\u003e of methods currently being inlined; if already present, return None (lower as a normal recursive call). Mirrors the MIR InliningPass `is_recursive` guard. A simple recursive enum / `inline copy()=this.copy()` / `@:using(self)` do NOT repro alone — needs a self-recursive STATIC abstract method.\n\nAfter Bug 1, the file compiles but `haxe.NativeStackTrace.callStack()`/`exceptionStack()` overflow at RUNTIME (rayzor_push_call_frame; func_id 300003 repeated 9000×). Bug = `fn@callStack` (CallStack.callStack, body `toHaxe(NativeStackTrace.callStack())`) calls ITSELF — MIR: `fn300003 = @callStack { ... $4 = call fn300003() ; ret toHaxe($4) }`.\n\n**TAST RESOLUTION IS CORRECT (verified by instrumenting resolve_class_method_symbol):** `NativeStackTrace.callStack()` resolves through Strategy 2 (exact qualified-name match) to the EXTERN symbol — `SymbolId(2048)`, qname `haxe.NativeStackTrace.callStack`, no body (not in class_methods). So FQN already works at TAST. The static-call path is ast_lowering.rs ~9059 (`resolve_class_method_symbol(class_symbol, method_name)`); resolve_method_symbol (~6943) is NOT used for this. resolve_class_method_symbol called 9× with class=NativeStackTrace → all hit Strategy 2 → correct extern.\n\n**Bug was HIR→MIR bare-name binding. FIXED 98f81a55.** The MIR had TWO `@callStack` fns: the NativeStackTrace extern and `CallStack.callStack` (real body). The call to extern SymbolId(2048) bound to CallStack.callStack by BARE NAME via `resolve_method_function_id`'s lone-match fallbacks (hir_to_mir ~1718/1729/1733) which returned a single same-named fn WITHOUT verifying its class. FIX: new `func_not_other_class()` guards those returns — reject a candidate whose qname positively names a DIFFERENT class (class = second-to-last segment of `pkg…Class.method`); conservative (allows when class unreadable). Rejected → None → caller falls through to the runtime-mapping dispatch. Result: 0 CRASHED (was infinite recursion), 141 PASSED, regression-free.\n\n`catch (e:MyException)` didn't catch a thrown MyException when a later `catch (e:Exception)` existed (Test 3 printed \"WRONG\"). Three misaligned encodings, all now on the deterministic class id (FNV-1a of qname) that object-header[0], runtime_type_id, and TYPE_REGISTRY use: (a) throw (hir_to_mir ~5549) read header[0] then added a stale +1000 → thrown=N+1000 vs catch-expected=N; drop the +1000. (b) rayzor_exception_type_matches subtracted 1000 before the hierarchy walk → keys miss the det-keyed registry; walk directly. (c) register_class_from_mir (cranelift_backend ~5254 + tiered_backend ~2610) registered super_type_id as the RAW TypeId.0 while keying by the deterministic id → parent walk (subclass-by-parent catch, instanceof/cast to base) missed; map raw→det (global across modules in cranelift) before registering. Verified: exact + hierarchy catch both work; 0 WRONG; suite 141 PASSED 0 FAILED.\n\nSIGILL(132) at Test 6/7 was a Cranelift TRAP (`udf #0xc11f` = CL's trap sentinel), NOT W0020. Diagnosed by mapping the trap PC to a function via RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP (per-pid /tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.\u003cpid\u003e.csv) under lldb (-k crash-hook to disassemble; addresses are deterministic across runs, no ASLR on the JIT mmap). Trap was the LAST instr of `Main.main` itself: `haxe.CallStack.callStack` got INLINED into main and execution hit a trap right after the inlined `native_stack_trace_call_stack()` call.\n\nTWO root bugs:\n(1) **Runtime-mapping key was bare \"NativeStackTrace\" but static-call dispatch provides the package-qualified \"haxe_NativeStackTrace\"** (qualified_name with '.'→'_', same convention as haxe_io_Bytes). So find_by_name_and_params(\"haxe_NativeStackTrace\",\"callStack\",0) → None (DBG-confirmed `found=false`), and `NativeStackTrace.callStack()` fell through to a bare-name bind onto `haxe.CallStack.callStack` (the WRONG class, returns Array\u003cStackItem\u003e via toHaxe). FIX: key the 4 mappings (saveStack/callStack/exceptionStack/toHaxe) by \"haxe_NativeStackTrace\" in runtime_mapping.rs:~4257.\n(2) **toHaxe(nativeStackTrace, skip:Int=0) called with 1 arg → cranelift arity-mismatch TRAP** (cranelift_backend.rs:~3486 emits `trap(user(1))` when call_args.len() != decl params). The `skip=0` default wasn't filled because the runtime-mapped extern path skips fill_default_args. FIX: in cranelift_backend, pad UNDER-arity calls (call_args \u003c expected) with zero/null of the exact declared scalar type instead of trapping (vector/ref params still trap). General — fixes any runtime-mapped extern with trailing defaults. Over-arity still traps.\n\nResult: `NativeStackTrace.callStack()`/`exceptionStack()` return non-null Strings (Test 6 \"has stack\", Test 7 \"has callstack\"); public `haxe.CallStack.callStack()` now works too (returns empty Array — toHaxe is a V1 stub). test_exception reaches \"done\" with all 7 tests coherent. Default-param sanity (greet) unaffected. Full suite 141 PASSED, only FAIL = test_copy_clone_identity exit-133 (documented W0020 flake, NOT a regression).\n\nRESIDUAL (was thought to be trap-stub-stranding) — ACTUALLY RESOLVED 2026-06-29, commit c643ec99. The ~20-40% intermittent exit-133 was NOT W0020/trap-stub-stranding. It was a **heap buffer overflow**: `MyException extends haxe.Exception` (an IMPORTED parent) was allocated UNDERSIZED. `register_class_metadata`'s `collect_inherited_fields` can't see an imported parent's fields, so `class_alloc_sizes[MyException]` covered only ~2 slots (16 bytes), but the `stack` field — written at `throw` and resolved via `resolve_field_index_by_name` (which DOES walk the parent chain) — sits at slot index 3 (offset 24). The throw's stack-field store wrote 8 bytes past the 16-byte block → corrupted the malloc free-list → `brk` in libsystem_malloc (EXC_BREAKPOINT/exit 133) on a later alloc. Intermittent because the trap depends on heap layout. FIX: `alloc_size_with_inheritance` (hir_to_mir.rs, near build_heap_alloc) walks the inheritance chain at the `new` site (all classes registered by then) and takes the max alloc size; larger alloc is always safe. Verified test_exception --no-cache 40/40, multi-catch repros 20/20, suite 153/153. Minimal repro = B3: `class MyException extends Exception{} ... catch(e:MyException){} catch(e:Exception){}`. Diagnosed via MIR dump (saw `call fn0(16)` alloc + `gep *void $5,[3]` write). Same heap-corruption CLASS as [[bugs_test_copy_clone_identity_flaky]] (also resolved this session) — neither was trap-stub-stranding; both were real memory bugs found by reading the MIR. Default-param-fill backend band-aid (Bug 4) still stands. RESIDUAL NOW: `Std.string(e)` on a Dynamic catch var holding a thrown String/primitive prints `\u003cunknown type N\u003e` — the thrown value isn't boxed as a DynamicValue at the catch binding (separate, open; typed `catch(e:String)` is a clean workaround).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_test_exception_two_bugs` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}